<p>When a storm or fire damages your home, the insurance estimate usually prices the cost to put back what was there. The problem is that the building department may not let you put back exactly what was there. Current <strong>building codes in McAllen</strong> can require upgrades during repairs, and those upgrades cost money. Whether your insurer pays for them is a separate question. This article explains how local codes, permits, and inspections interact with insurance repairs, and where the money for code work can come from.</p>

<p>For a reference on the codes themselves, permits, and city contacts, see our <a href="/local-building-codes/">McAllen local building codes</a> page.</p>

<h2>What codes McAllen currently enforces</h2>

<p>The City of McAllen adopted the <strong>2024 International Code Council (ICC) codes</strong>, effective January 1, 2026. According to the city's materials, that includes:</p>

<ul>
<li>International Residential Code (IRC) 2024</li>
<li>International Building Code (IBC) 2024</li>
<li>International Existing Building Code (IEBC) 2024</li>
<li>International Energy Conservation Code (IECC) 2024</li>
<li>The 2024 plumbing, mechanical, fuel gas, fire, swimming pool and spa, and wildland-urban interface codes</li>
<li>The 2023 National Electrical Code (NEC)</li>
</ul>

<p>The city adopted these with local exceptions, including interior residential lighting controls, a commercial adult changing-table requirement, and municipal residential fire sprinkler requirements. The details are on our <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes page</a> and with the city's <a href="https://www.mcallen.net/departments/permits/home" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Building Permits &amp; Inspections department</a>.</p>

<p>Why does the adoption date matter for insurance? Many homes in McAllen were built under much older codes. When a covered loss forces significant repairs, the work is generally permitted and inspected under the codes in effect when the permit is issued, not the codes in effect when the house was built. The gap between the two is where code-upgrade costs come from.</p>

<h2>Permits and inspections in McAllen</h2>

<p>Here are the permit facts that most often come up during insurance repairs:</p>

