</tbody>
</table>

<p>The permit timing rules are worth noting for insurance claims. If a claim drags on, a permit pulled early may expire before work starts. Coordinate the permit with the claim timeline, especially if you are waiting on a supplement or appraisal. Our <a href="/local-building-codes/">building codes reference page</a> keeps the city contacts and permit links in one place.</p>

<h2>Code compliance does not automatically create coverage</h2>

<p>This is the point many homeowners and contractors miss. A building inspector can require a code upgrade, and the work may be unavoidable to pass inspection. That does not, by itself, obligate your insurer to pay for it. What the insurer owes depends on your policy.</p>

<p>In general, there are three ways code-related costs end up in a claim:</p>

<ol>
<li><strong>As part of the normal cost to repair.</strong> Some items are simply part of doing the repair properly under current standards and may already be built into how the work is priced. Whether an insurer accepts that depends on the item, the policy, and the evidence.</li>
<li><strong>Through ordinance or law coverage.</strong> This coverage pays, up to its limit, for increased costs to comply with codes when repairing covered damage.</li>
<li><strong>Not covered.</strong> If the policy has no ordinance or law coverage, or the limit is exhausted, code upgrade costs beyond the normal repair may fall on the owner.</li>
</ol>

<div class="callout callout-gold"><span class="callout-title">Read your declarations page</span><p>Look for "ordinance or law," "building ordinance," or "code upgrade" on your declarations page or endorsements. Note the limit, which is often a percentage of your dwelling coverage, and whether it is included or added by endorsement.</p></div>

<h2>How ordinance or law coverage works</h2>

<p>Ordinance or law coverage is often a separate, limited coverage or endorsement. Policy forms vary, but common features include:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>A separate limit.</strong> Often stated as a percentage of Coverage A (dwelling). Once that limit is used up, it pays no more for code upgrades.</li>
<li><strong>Tied to covered damage.</strong> It typically applies to code costs triggered by repairing damage the policy covers, not to upgrades on undamaged parts of the house unless the code requires it as part of the covered repair.</li>
<li><strong>Payment after work is done.</strong> Many policies pay code upgrade amounts only after the work is completed, similar to recoverable depreciation under replacement-cost policies.</li>
<li><strong>Documentation.</strong> Insurers usually want proof the code requirement applies, such as the permit, an inspector's correction notice, or a written statement from the contractor citing the requirement.</li>
</ul>

<p>For how payment timing works on replacement-cost policies more broadly, see <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value in Texas</a>.</p>

