<h2>Code items commonly disputed in roof and repair estimates</h2>

<p>Code requirements for roofing, such as underlayment, drip edge, fastening, and design wind loads, come from the adopted IRC and IBC and the standards they reference. We are not going to quote specific numeric requirements here, because the exact requirement depends on the code section, the roof type, and your property. What we can say is which items tend to come up in disputes.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Why it gets disputed</th><th>What helps</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Drip edge</td><td>Older roofs often have none; the estimate may not include it for a re-roof</td><td>Contractor statement citing the code requirement for the roof scope; inspector notes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Underlayment</td><td>Estimate may price a lighter or different underlayment than the code or manufacturer requires</td><td>Manufacturer installation instructions and code citation from the contractor</td></tr>
<tr><td>Decking (sheathing) replacement</td><td>Damaged, deteriorated, or improperly spaced decking may need replacement before new roofing can be installed, but is rarely visible until tear-off</td><td>Photos during tear-off, measurements, and a prompt supplement request before the decking is covered up</td></tr>
<tr><td>Roof ventilation</td><td>Re-roofing may require ventilation to meet current requirements; estimates may only replace existing vents</td><td>Contractor's ventilation calculation and code reference</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flashing</td><td>Reusing old flashing may not be appropriate or permitted with the new roof system</td><td>Photos of existing flashing condition; manufacturer and code requirements</td></tr>
<tr><td>Electrical upgrades</td><td>Opening walls after fire or water damage can trigger requirements under the 2023 NEC for the repaired area</td><td>Electrician's written scope and inspector correction notices</td></tr>
<tr><td>Plumbing upgrades</td><td>Replacing damaged lines or fixtures may require current-code materials or methods</td><td>Plumber's scope and permit documentation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Permit fees</td><td>Sometimes omitted from the estimate entirely</td><td>Copy of the permit and fee receipt</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>A note on what is not typical here: ice and water shield requirements tied to ice dams are a big topic in colder parts of the country, but they are not a common code issue for McAllen roofs. If you see an argument built on cold-climate requirements, ask how it applies to a Rio Grande Valley home.</p>

<h3>Decking: the item to catch before it is covered up</h3>

<p>Decking problems usually only show up once the old shingles come off. If the roofer finds damaged or noncompliant decking during tear-off, stop and document it: photos, measurements, the number of sheets affected, and a written explanation. Then notify the insurer promptly and request a supplement. Once new roofing goes on, the evidence is gone. Our article on <a href="/hail-damage-claim-supplements/">hail damage claim supplements</a> covers this timing in more detail.</p>

<h3>Interior repairs after fire or water damage</h3>

<p>Code issues are not limited to roofs. After a fire or a large water loss, opening walls and ceilings can expose wiring, plumbing, or framing that must be brought up to current standards for the repaired area. Insulation and energy code requirements under the 2024 IECC may also come into play when walls are opened. Get the electrician's, plumber's, or general contractor's scope in writing, and keep any correction notices from city inspectors. See our <a href="/services/fire-damage-claims/">fire damage claims</a> and <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a> pages for more.</p>

<h2>A practical process for handling code items on your claim</h2>

<ol>
<li><strong>Check your coverage first.</strong> Find out whether you have ordinance or law coverage and its limit.</li>
<li><strong>Ask your contractor to identify code-required items in writing,</strong> with the specific code section or the reason the inspector requires it. Vague notes like "code upgrade" are easy for an insurer to question.</li>
<li><strong>Pull the permit</strong> through the city and keep copies of the permit, inspection results, and any correction notices.</li>
<li><strong>Submit code items to the insurer</strong> as part of the estimate or as a supplement, with the documentation attached.</li>
<li><strong>Document hidden conditions</strong> (decking, wiring, framing) before they are covered.</li>
<li><strong>Keep invoices</strong> showing the code work was performed, since many policies pay this portion after completion.</li>
<li><strong>Escalate if needed.</strong> If the insurer agrees the damage is covered but disputes the amount, appraisal may be available. Coverage disputes, such as whether ordinance or law coverage applies at all, are a different matter; consult a Texas attorney for legal questions.</li>
</ol>

<p>If you are comparing the insurer's estimate with your contractor's, our guide on <a href="/how-to-review-underpaid-roof-insurance-estimate/">how to review an underpaid roof insurance estimate</a> shows how to read the line items. You may also find our <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a> service helpful.</p>

<h2>How a public adjuster fits in</h2>

<p>A public adjuster does not perform repairs. Under Texas Insurance Code Chapter 4102, a public adjuster may not participate in the repair or reconstruction of a property on a claim they adjust. What a public adjuster can do is review your policy for ordinance or law coverage, work with the code requirements your contractor and the city identify, document them in the claim, and present them to the insurer in a format it can evaluate. Joseph Dittman, a licensed Texas public adjuster with hands-on construction experience, handles this kind of work for property owners in McAllen and the surrounding Rio Grande Valley (RGV).</p>

<p>Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. Revisit the <a href="/local-building-codes/">McAllen building codes reference</a> before you start repairs.</p>
